model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "A signed usage summary reports each of its statements as a history_summary_phase line. Any signed history read that answers 5xx logs one POOL_USAGE_READ_FAILED line with its action, status, failure and the statement that failed, and reports pool_usage_read_failed to error tracking, so a failing overview read alerts instead of answering a bare 503."
      constraint "Percentage notifications require a breached one-hour or twenty-four-hour window with at least one hundred real requests in that same window and a real failure in the latest five-minute window. Notification eligibility never rewrites the raw success rate, counts or objective state. The pool app displays the raw one-hour and twenty-four-hour real success rates and counts independently of recent incidents, and annotates nonempty samples below one hundred real requests. Its existing optional reliability read batches both windows in one statement and preserves the usage snapshot on failure. Proof traffic cannot meet the volume or recency conditions. An objective that unknown outcomes leave unavailable raises the collection incident only when a window they leave undecided has at least one hundred real requests, counting the unknown ones it never counted; the figure, its counts and the per-check-in unknown-outcome log line stay as they are, and every other reason the objective is unavailable still raises it. Immediate real-failure, synthetic availability and collection-integrity signals keep their independent behavior."
      constraint "The overlapping five-minute, one-hour and twenty-four-hour SLO windows aggregate one materialized request selection in the caller's existing snapshot. Raw request times retain nanosecond text boundaries; terminal finishes retain their timestamp boundaries. Global latest-attempt selection, terminal-only fallback, proof classification and unavailable-collection rules remain identical in each window."
      constraint "Request reliability counts only the retained local model and gateway sources. Explicit purpose records match the exact pool, source and hashed client key; unclassified keys count as real. Proof counts remain visible separately, while token and cost totals retain every request. The same SQL read supplies the overview and rolling five-minute, one-hour and twenty-four-hour request windows. Empty real traffic has no success rate. Optional reliability reads use a savepoint so an unavailable monitor cannot discard the usage, token or cost answers. Availability RPC failure retains an independently measured reliability verdict and both closed failure classes. An inactive, stale, lagging or unclassified capture cannot establish the objective. The once-per-minute availability check-in reports only the synthetic pool probe. Real-request success below 99 percent emits its own error through the existing observability route. Missing collection remains unknown in the interface and emits a separate reliability incident with bounded counts and a closed reason. It never becomes an availability outage. Recent real failures emit their own signal even when the longer success objective is met. Empty real traffic and proof-only failures do not report an outage. Before dispatch, the Worker assigns a request identity and records metadata-only start and terminal evidence. Home usage remains append only. Video job status remains separate from successful HTTP receipt or poll delivery. Terminal failure overlays the final matching Home attempt; finished requests awaiting Home collection count once and disappear from that fallback when usage arrives. Missing terminal tables cannot establish the objective. A request whose outcome no record establishes (a start older than ten minutes with no finish, a finish without an observable outcome, or a Home row with no finished start) counts as a possible failure: a window breaches only if its known failures break the target even when every such request succeeded, meets it only if it holds even when every such request failed, and otherwise leaves the objective unavailable; a breach in either window still wins. Each is weighed in the window that counts its latest Home attempt, where one already counted as failed stays one failure, or at its start or finish when it has no Home attempt, adding to that window's requests. Proof traffic's lost records do not count. Those requests are counted per window and class in the figure, its alert context and a log line on every check-in that has any, each read bounded by a time index or a key. The percentage page fires only from a window that is itself breached. Counts and closed reasons leave the service; keys and request content do not. Terminal observation selects ArrayBuffer delivery on both accepted WebSocket peers before forwarding or inspecting binary frames. Runtime Blob defaults must not change payload bytes, hide request starts or discard private consumer receipts. Response adapters preserve the private model lifetime binding, including selected and legacy Responses sockets. Terminal collection readiness and the correlation cutover use the named v7 marker independently of later import completion migrations."
      constraint "Release provisioning applies the additive named traffic-purpose schema through the shared model-history migrator before Worker activation. Migration failure blocks activation; the private reviewed schema operation uses the same runner. It never rewrites request history. Purpose changes use the private operations entrypoint with a dry run listing exact retained request identifiers, current classifications and an evidence hash. Applying requires the digest of that selection; a changed selection refuses. A started local receipt prevents replay after an unknown apply outcome. Restoring a proof key to real uses the same reviewed path. Public app and model routes cannot change purpose."
      constraint "A gateway refusal records its history row under the attempt's request identity, which the request's terminal evidence and Home usage share. A runtime endpoint's correlation id keys the refusal's log line, error event and client-visible request id, never its history row; the log line also carries the attempt's identity. Terminal evidence counts a request only when no retained Home attempt or gateway refusal already holds a row with its identity, so a retried request refused before dispatch counts once. The reliability windows read the requests that finished in them once; a latest Home attempt takes its finish from that read, only a latest attempt without one there is looked up by key, and only a finish of a request with no Home attempt is checked for a refusal, since that check reads every request partition."
    }
  }
  basalt.telemetry -[sync]-> basalt.pool "read request reliability contracts for monitor diagnostics"
}
